::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which habit is most important before solving a math problem? {
~A. Write down the first operation you notice.#This can lead to mistakes because the first number or operation may not match what the problem asks.
=B. Read the problem carefully and identify what it asks.#Correct. Reading first helps you choose a strategy that matches the question.
~C. Skip the question if it has words.#Word problems still provide mathematical information. Start by identifying what is being asked.
~D. Use the largest number as the answer.#The largest number may be part of the problem, but it is rarely the answer by itself.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student has 16 completed tasks and 7 tasks left. How many tasks are there in all? {
~A. 9 tasks#This subtracts 7 from 16, but the phrase "in all" asks for a total.
~B. 112 tasks#This multiplies the numbers, but the situation is not equal groups.
=C. 23 tasks#Correct. Add completed and remaining tasks: 16 + 7 = 23.
~D. 27 tasks#This is a computation error. Add 16 + 7 carefully to get 23.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 6 + 5 &times; 2. {
~A. 22#This adds first and then multiplies. Multiplication comes before addition.
~B. 13#This may come from adding only some of the numbers. Follow the order of operations.
~C. 60#This multiplies all three numbers, but the expression includes addition and multiplication.
=D. 16#Correct. First 5 &times; 2 = 10. Then 6 + 10 = 16.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 18 - 6 &divide; 3. {
=A. 16#Correct. First 6 &divide; 3 = 2. Then 18 - 2 = 16.
~B. 4#This subtracts first and then divides. Division must be completed before subtraction.
~C. 6#This ignores part of the expression after division. Complete 6 &divide; 3 first, then subtract from 18.
~D. 12#This subtracts 6 and ignores the division by 3. The division belongs to 6 &divide; 3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A notebook costs $4. A student buys 5 notebooks. How much does the student spend? {
~A. $9#This adds 4 and 5, but the situation has 5 equal groups of $4.
~B. $10#This does not match the cost per notebook or the number of notebooks.
=C. $20#Correct. Five notebooks at $4 each means 5 &times; 4 = 20 dollars.
~D. $45#This joins the digits instead of using an operation that matches the context.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which answer best explains why 12 &times; 3 = 36 is reasonable? {
~A. 12 and 3 are both numbers in the problem.#Using numbers from the problem is not enough. The operation and result must match the context.
=B. 12 &times; 3 means three groups of 12, and 36 is close to the estimate 10 &times; 3 = 30.#Correct. The explanation connects multiplication to equal groups and uses an estimate to check reasonableness.
~C. 36 is reasonable because it is the largest answer.#The largest answer is not always reasonable. Reasonableness depends on the context and estimate.
~D. Multiplication always makes answers correct.#Multiplication is only correct when it matches the situation.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student estimates 48 + 31 as about 80. Is this estimate reasonable? {
=A. Yes, because 48 is close to 50 and 31 is close to 30.#Correct. Rounding gives 50 + 30 = 80, so the estimate is reasonable.
~B. Yes, because 48 + 31 is exactly 80.#The exact sum is 79, not 80. The estimate is reasonable because it is close.
~C. No, because estimates must be exact.#Estimates are not exact; they are used to check whether an answer makes sense.
~D. No, because addition cannot be estimated.#Addition can be estimated by rounding numbers to friendly values.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response shows the clearest mathematical communication? {
~A. 24#A number alone may be correct, but it does not show reasoning or meaning.
~B. I did it in my head.#This does not show enough work for another person to follow the reasoning.
~C. The answer is obvious.#This does not explain the mathematical reasoning.
=D. 6 &times; 4 = 24, so there are 24 total items.#Correct. This shows the operation, result, and meaning.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student evaluates 9 + 2 &times; 6 as 66. What misconception is shown? {
~A. The student rounded before calculating.#No rounding is shown. The error is about order of operations.
~B. The student multiplied before adding.#Multiplying before adding is the correct order for this expression.
=C. The student added before multiplying.#Correct. The student likely did 9 + 2 first, then multiplied by 6.
~D. The student used subtraction instead of addition.#There is no subtraction in the student's work.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the correct value of 9 + 2 &times; 6? {
~A. 66#This repeats the error of adding before multiplying.
=B. 21#Correct. First 2 &times; 6 = 12. Then 9 + 12 = 21.
~C. 54#This multiplies 9 by 6 and does not follow the expression.
~D. 17#This appears to add all numbers without using multiplication correctly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student has 40 points and needs 100 points. How many more points are needed? {
~A. 140 points#This adds, but the question asks for how many more points are needed.
~B. 50 points#This is an estimate, but the problem asks for the exact number of points needed.
~C. 4000 points#This multiplies the numbers, which does not match the phrase "how many more."
=D. 60 points#Correct. Find the difference: 100 - 40 = 60.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase usually tells you to find a total? {
=A. In all#Correct. "In all" usually means combine quantities to find a total.
~B. How many more#This usually points to subtraction or comparison.
~C. How much less#This usually points to subtraction or comparison.
~D. Difference between#This usually means subtract to compare two quantities.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase usually tells you to compare by subtraction? {
~A. Altogether#This usually asks for a total.
~B. In all#This usually asks for a total.
=C. How many more#Correct. "How many more" asks for the difference between two quantities.
~D. Each group#This often points toward multiplication or division with equal groups.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes 7 + 8 = 15 for "7 boxes with 8 pencils in each box." What should the student do instead? {
~A. Keep 15 because both numbers were used.#Using both numbers is not enough; the operation must match the context.
=B. Use 7 &times; 8 because the situation has equal groups.#Correct. Seven boxes with eight pencils each means 7 equal groups of 8.
~C. Use 8 - 7 because the numbers are close.#The problem does not ask for a difference.
~D. Use 78 because the numbers appear in that order.#Joining digits does not represent the relationship in the situation.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the best reason to show work in a mastery-based math course? {
=A. It lets the Teacher of Record see the reasoning and identify support needs.#Correct. Clear work helps show both understanding and learning gaps.
~B. It makes the page look longer.#The purpose of showing work is not length. It is to make reasoning visible.
~C. It replaces the need for an answer.#An answer is still needed. Work explains how the answer was found.
~D. It guarantees every answer is correct.#Showing work helps, but mistakes can still happen and need correction.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes error analysis? {
~A. Erasing all work and starting over without explanation.#This hides the thinking and does not explain the misconception.
=B. Finding the mistake, explaining why it happened, and correcting it.#Correct. Error analysis turns a mistake into useful learning.
~C. Marking an answer wrong and moving on.#This does not help you learn from the mistake.
~D. Changing the answer until it matches a key.#A correction should include reasoning, not just a changed answer.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student evaluates 30 - 12 &divide; 6 as 3. What is the correct value? {
~A. 3#This repeats the error of subtracting before dividing.
~B. 5#This comes from dividing 30 by 6 and ignoring part of the expression.
~C. 18#This subtracts 12 and ignores the division by 6.
=D. 28#Correct. First 12 &divide; 6 = 2. Then 30 - 2 = 28.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which answer is most reasonable for 19 + 22? {
=A. 41#Correct. 19 + 22 = 41, and an estimate of 20 + 20 = 40 supports that it is reasonable.
~B. 4#This is far too small for adding two numbers near 20.
~C. 90#This is too large because both numbers are only a little above or below 20.
~D. 418#This joins or misuses digits instead of adding the quantities.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which strategy best checks whether 58 + 21 = 79 is reasonable? {
~A. Multiply 58 by 21.#Multiplication does not check the reasonableness of this addition problem.
~B. Ignore the 21 because 58 is larger.#Both numbers are part of the sum.
=C. Round to 60 + 20 and compare the estimate to 79.#Correct. 60 + 20 = 80, which is close to 79.
~D. Subtract 21 from 58 and compare.#Subtraction answers a different question.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A readiness diagnostic is mainly used to: {
~A. give a final course grade.#A diagnostic is used before or during learning to identify needs, not to determine the final grade.
=B. identify strengths and gaps before instruction continues.#Correct. A diagnostic helps guide learning and support.
~C. skip all future lessons.#A diagnostic may show strengths, but it does not replace the course.
~D. prove that mistakes are not allowed.#Mistakes are useful information during a diagnostic.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best shows student responsibility after missing a readiness question? {
=A. Review the feedback, correct the work, and note the skill that needs practice.#Correct. This uses feedback to guide improvement.
~B. Ignore it because it was only one question.#Ignoring errors can leave skill gaps unaddressed.
~C. Change the answer without reviewing the work.#Changing an answer is not enough; the misconception needs attention.
~D. Wait for someone else to solve every similar problem.#Support is helpful, but students still need to engage with corrections.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the answer to "4 packs with 6 markers in each pack" is 10. What misconception is most likely? {
~A. The student multiplied instead of adding.#Multiplication is the correct operation for equal groups.
~B. The student estimated correctly.#The answer 10 is not a reasonable estimate for 4 groups of 6.
~C. The student used the correct operation and made no mistake.#The correct total is 24 markers, so there is a mistake.
=D. The student added instead of multiplying equal groups.#Correct. Four packs of six markers means 4 &times; 6, not 4 + 6.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the correct answer to "4 packs with 6 markers in each pack"? {
~A. 10 markers#This adds 4 and 6, but the situation has equal groups.
~B. 12 markers#This does not account for all 4 packs of 6 markers.
=C. 24 markers#Correct. Four packs with six markers each means 4 &times; 6 = 24 markers.
~D. 46 markers#This joins the numbers instead of multiplying equal groups.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains a reasonable answer? {
=A. It makes sense based on the numbers, operation, and context.#Correct. Reasonableness depends on the full situation.
~B. It is always the exact answer.#A reasonableness check often uses estimation and may not be exact.
~C. It is the answer that uses the largest number.#The largest number is not automatically reasonable.
~D. It is any answer written with a unit.#Units help, but the value must also make sense.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which skill from this lesson supports all future Algebra 1 lessons? {
~A. Memorizing answers without showing work.#Algebra requires reasoning, not just memorized answers.
~B. Choosing operations randomly.#Operations must match the structure of the problem.
~C. Avoiding corrections after mistakes.#Corrections are part of mastery-based learning.
=D. Explaining reasoning clearly and checking answers for reasonableness.#Correct. Clear reasoning and checking work support every later Algebra 1 topic.
}
